{
  "dom_id": "step-q102335",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =x\\cdot {q}_{3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$",
